  </div>

  <!-- PART I -->
  <div id="part1">
    <span class="part-tag">Part I · 101 First Principles</span>
    <h2>1. Excitable Membranes as Local Active-Pair Networks</h2>
    <p>Every neuron begins with electrochemistry across a nanometer lipid bilayer. Unequal ionic concentrations ($\text{Na}^+$, $\text{K}^+$, $\text{Cl}^-$) create a Nernst equilibrium potential for each ion species $s$ of valence $z_s$:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$E_s = \frac{RT}{z_s F} \ln\frac{[s]_{\text{out}}}{[s]_{\text{in}}}, \qquad C_m \frac{dV}{dt} = I_{\text{ext}} - \sum_s g_s(V, t)\,(V - E_s)$$</div>
    <p>In the 4-dimensional Hodgkin–Huxley model, voltage-gated $\text{Na}^+$ activation ($m$), $\text{Na}^+$ inactivation ($h$), and $\text{K}^+$ activation ($n$) obey first-order local gating kinetics. Because $m$ equilibrates an order of magnitude faster than $(h, n)$, the excitability core reduces cleanly to the 2-dimensional <strong>FitzHugh–Nagumo (FHN)</strong> interaction pair between fast membrane voltage $v$ and slow recovery $w$:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\frac{dv}{dt} = v - \frac{v^3}{3} - w + I_{\text{ext}}, \qquad \frac{dw}{dt} = \varepsilon\,(v + a - b\,w)$$</div>
    <p>Viewed structurally, this is a <strong>local interaction network</strong>: each neuron is a bounded state-bearing cell whose principal port exchanges spike events with neighboring synapses; no cell ever inspects global network state. When an external current $I_{\text{ext}}$ pushes the $(v, w)$ equilibrium across the Hopf bifurcation, the active pair enters a self-sustaining limit cycle — guarded corecursion in wetware.</p>

    <div class="lab-card">
      <div class="lab-head">
        <span class="lbl">◈ Interactive Lab 1 · Driven FitzHugh–Nagumo Phase-Space &amp; SOS Trapping Envelope</span>
        <span class="lab-status" id="fhnStatus">Certified Trapping Region: dV/dt &lt; 0 outside R</span>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-stage" id="fhnStage"><canvas id="fhnCanvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="lab-ctrl">
        <label>Drive $I_{\text{ext}}$: <input id="fhnI" type="range" min="-0.4" max="1.2" step="0.05" value="0.55"><span id="fhnIVal">0.55</span></label>
        <label>Recovery $b$: <input id="fhnB" type="range" min="0.4" max="1.4" step="0.05" value="0.80"><span id="fhnBVal">0.80</span></label>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="fhnPerturb">Inject Perturbation</button>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="fhnReset">Reset</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PART II -->
  <div id="part2">
    <span class="part-tag">Part II · Modern Research Frontier</span>
    <h2>2. Intrinsic Manifolds, Output-Null Fibers &amp; Closed-Loop Two-Learner Co-Adaptation</h2>
    <p>Across motor, prefrontal, and sensory cortex, populations of $N \sim 10^4$ neurons do not wander freely through $\mathbb{R}^N$. Synaptic coupling restricts population activity $x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^N$ to a low-dimensional <strong>intrinsic manifold</strong> $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathbb{R}^N$ ($d \ll N$), while a linear or nonlinear readout $y = C x$ projects $\mathcal{M}$ to motor or BCI command space $\mathbb{R}^k$.</p>
    <p>The preimage of a target command $y$ is an <strong>output-null fiber</strong> $\mathrm{Fib}_C(y) = x_0 + \ker C$. Motor preparation, internal rehearsal, and neural variability live inside $\ker C$ without moving the actuator — the exact same observation-fiber geometry formalised in <code>neuro/descent.hypercubical</code> and <code>lib/observation.hypercubical</code>.</p>

    <h3>The Two-Learner Co-Adaptation Instability</h3>
    <p>In a closed-loop brain–machine interface (the Berkeley two-learner lineage: Carmena, Orsborn, Burden, Madduri), <em>both</em> the biological cortex ($B$) and the electronic co-processor ($C$) adapt online to minimize task error $(e_B, e_C)$:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\frac{de_B}{dt} = -\eta_B e_B + \gamma_1 e_C, \qquad \frac{de_C}{dt} = \gamma_2 e_B - \eta_C e_C$$</div>
    <p>Here $\eta_B, \eta_C &gt; 0$ are the self-stabilizing learning rates of brain and decoder, and $\gamma_1, \gamma_2$ are the cross-adaptation couplings. If the co-processor adapts too aggressively relative to biological plasticity ($\eta_B \eta_C \le \gamma_1 \gamma_2$), the two-node interaction network destabilizes and diverges — a catastrophic failure mode in implanted BCIs.</p>

    <div class="lab-card">
      <div class="lab-head">
        <span class="lbl">◈ Interactive Lab 2 · Two-Learner Brain ↔ Co-Processor Interaction Network</span>
        <span class="lab-status" id="tlStatus">Δ = η_B·η_C − γ₁γ₂ = +0.52 (CERTIFIED STABLE)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-stage" id="tlStage"><canvas id="tlCanvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="lab-ctrl">
        <label>Brain $\eta_B$: <input id="tlEb" type="range" min="0.1" max="1.6" step="0.05" value="0.90"><span id="tlEbV">0.90</span></label>
        <label>Decoder $\eta_C$: <input id="tlEc" type="range" min="0.1" max="1.6" step="0.05" value="0.85"><span id="tlEcV">0.85</span></label>
        <label>Coupling $\gamma$: <input id="tlGm" type="range" min="0.0" max="1.4" step="0.05" value="0.50"><span id="tlGmV">0.50</span></label>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="tlKick">Perturb Error</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PART III -->
  <div id="part3">
    <span class="part-tag">Part III · Computational Closure in Hyperactivity</span>
    <h2>3. Exact Symbolic SOS Lyapunov &amp; Control Barrier Certificates Over $\mathbb{Q}$</h2>
    <p>Numerical simulation can test a million trajectories and still miss the one parameter corner that injures a patient. Hyperactivity closes the verification gap by deciding polynomial <strong>Sum-of-Squares (SOS)</strong> identities in exact normal form over the universal polynomial ring $\mathbb{Q}[x_1, \dots, x_n]$ via <code>chem/algebra.hypercubical</code> (<code>al-id</code>) — so every stability and safety proof holds for <em>all</em> real parameter values simultaneously, with zero floating-point approximation.</p>

    <div class="thm-box">
      <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem · Asymmetric Two-Learner Weighted Lyapunov SOS</span><span><code>neuro/lyapunov.hypercubical</code></span></div>
      <p>For weighted Lyapunov energy $V(e_B, e_C) = p_1 e_B^2 + p_2 e_C^2$ ($p_1, p_2 &gt; 0$), the dissipation rate satisfies the exact universal ring identity checked by <code>two-learner-asym-sos-id</code>:</p>
      <div class="eq-box">$$4 p_1 \eta_B \left(-\frac{dV}{dt}\right) = \big(2 p_1 \eta_B e_B - (p_1 \gamma_1 + p_2 \gamma_2)\,e_C\big)^2 + \Big(4 p_1 p_2 \eta_B \eta_C - (p_1 \gamma_1 + p_2 \gamma_2)^2\Big)\,e_C^2$$</div>
      <p>Choosing $p_1 \gamma_1 = p_2 \gamma_2$ recovers the sharp Hurwitz stability boundary $\eta_B \eta_C &gt; \gamma_1 \gamma_2$ as a single constructive square decomposition.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="thm-box">
      <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem · Forward-Invariant Control Barrier Functions</span><span><code>neuro/barrier.hypercubical</code></span></div>
      <p>For a safe set $\mathcal{C} = \{x : h(x) \ge 0\}$, Nagumo's theorem guarantees forward invariance whenever $\dot{h}(x) + \alpha h(x) \ge 0$. Both the two-learner safety barrier <code>cbf-2learner-safe-sos-id</code> and the externally driven FitzHugh–Nagumo trapping region <code>fhn-driven-trapping-sos-id</code> are decided as exact SOS identities over $\mathbb{Q}$: no bounded external current $I_{\text{ext}}$ can drive membrane dynamics outside the certified ellipsoid.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Interface-Reshaping Co-Processors, Proof-Gated Activation &amp; Cognitive Inhibition</h2>
    <p>Real neural interfaces are not static wiring diagrams: channels open and close, decoders re-bind, and sensory modalities come online mid-run. In <code>lib/polyproc.hypercubical</code>, Hyperactivity generalizes guarded corecursive processes to <strong>indexed interface-reshaping processes</strong> <code>RProc</code> over a dynamic signature <code>DynSig</code>, with an exact cubical round-trip equivalence <code>gproc-rproc-id</code> back to static processes.</p>
    <p>In <code>neuro/reshaping-coproc.hypercubical</code> and <code>neuro/coproc.hypercubical</code>, stimulation or actuation is gated by a proof-carrying transition <code>SafeGateDS</code>: the theorem <code>disagreement-blocks-gate</code> proves that whenever any two histories in the co-processor's observation fiber disagree on safety, the gate is structurally blocked from firing. Simultaneously, <code>neuro/inhibition.hypercubical</code> formalizes cognitive inhibition and REBUS prior relaxation as fiber-widening maps, and <code>neuro/synesthesia.hypercubical</code> formalizes grapheme-color synthetic synesthesia as an exact structural homomorphism on the interaction network.</p>
  </div>

